    codeTask:
        prompt:
            \\\
            You're still not getting the scrolling between message and task history.  One big scroll, it'll kinda pause at the top of either screen but a continuous scroll should after a little extra perform the switch.  You *don't* have to stop the scroll and restart.
            \\\
        result:
            \\\
            I've changed the spec only, not the code. `piton check`, `piton format --check` and `piton build` all pass.

            **Why it doesn't switch today.** The spec already said one continuous scroll should cross over, but the code keeps draining the build-up while you're still scrolling. In `prompt_mode.rs`, `ScrollPush::held` starts draining from each scroll step, spread over 500 ms. On Wayland one wheel notch is 3 lines, about 60 px. If you spin the wheel steadily at 150–200 ms per notch, the build-up levels off around 150–200 px and never reaches the 200 px barrier. So it sits at the top until you stop and start a new scroll. I found this by reading the code; I haven't reproduced it in the app.

            **Changes to `spec/scope/prompt-mode/message-list/Scrolling.pi`, in `MessageListScrollingShape.scrollingPast`:**
            - **No draining while scrolling:** a scroll counts as still going while its steps arrive less than 200 ms apart. Each step adds all of itself to the build-up, so a steady scroll at any speed reaches the barrier and crosses over.
            - **Drain after stopping:** the build-up only starts draining 200 ms after the last step, and is back to nothing within 500 ms of it. A small nudge into the stretch still never crosses over later.
            - **Overshoot counts:** when a scroll step reaches the end, whatever part of it goes past the end starts the build-up instead of being lost.
            - **Momentum:** only scrolling after the platform reports the fingers lifting counts as momentum. Wayland never reports this, so on Linux nothing counts as momentum.
            - **Notch count:** 200 px is now described as about four wheel notches, not two.

            **Anchor the code should follow:** `MessageListScrollingShape.scrollingPast`. The code changes are:
            - Change `ScrollPush::held` and the stretch pull so they hold at full value until 200 ms after the last step, then drain to nothing by 500 ms. This needs a new constant next to `SCROLL_DRAIN`.
            - In `scroll_past_ends`, add the part of a step that goes past the end to the build-up. The barrier, the carry-on-from-slide rule and the 80 ms rest all stay as they are.
            \\\

    sentFrom: Prompt_113f8b225993f74f
